    IMHO,(and I am no expert on Quantum Interpretation), the whole notion
of wavefunction "collapse" is wrong. It gives the appearance that the
wavefunction is "real" in a sense. The Many-Worlds Interpretion is more
appealing because it does away with the collapse postulate. 

  Wavefunction collapse has led to all sorts of new age literature based on
observer created reality. I think this all started with Von Neumann who tried
to determine where the collapse happened. He examined each component in
an experimental setup and found that at each stage the wavefunction
continued (if a measurement apparatus observed an event, the apparatus is
now in a superposition of states just like live-undead cats). He found he
could place the uncollapsed wave function arbitrarily close to the human
observer, so the only likely place for the collapse to happen was in the mind.

*blue-sky mode*
    I would speculate that an observation "collapses" the wavefunction once
it becomes "non-erasable". Once information from the result of an observation
becomes irreversibly transmitted, the wave function is irreversibly
collapsed. The second law and information theory probably has some deep
connections with collapse.
*blue-sky off*


