Date: Sun, 7 Mar 1993 21:59:39 -0500 (EST)
From: root@rmsdell.ftl.fl.us (Yanek Martinson)
Subject: FEDS: internet monitoring

> How private is e-mail when I send the letter and it goes straight 
> from here to the recipient's machine via TCP-IP protocol?

Not very.  If you don't use encryption, then there are quite a few
points at which the message could be intercepted.

The most obvious are the originating and destination hosts.

The editor in which you are typing your message may be saving some
temporary files.  The MTA (mail transfer agent, typically _sendmail_)
creates spool files for the mail queue.  Even non-root users can use
the 'mailq' command to see where mail is going to.  Someone with root
privileges can of course examine the actual spool files for the
complete text.  Some mail programs such as smail keep logs, which list
the date/time, size of the message, and originator and recipient. 

On the receiving end, again there can be a mail program that creates
logs.  Then, before you read your message, it is stored in a mailbox
file, which can be read by someone with the appropriate access.

Even if the two machines are private workstations, the message could
be intercepted while it is being transmitted between them.  If you
have the _traceroute_ command, you can see how many hosts and routers
your packet travels through before reaching its destination.  At each
point it could be logged or searched for some keywords.  Currently a
very large percentage of internet traffic crosses at some point the
nsf routers, which would be one logical point for someone to tap if
they were interested in monitoring.  A few other strategically placed
taps at endpoints of the connections across atlantic and the pacific,
plus some satellites, and it should be possible to capture almost all
of the information traveling through the net.  

It is very probable that this is being done.  

The best way to guard against such monitoring is to use encryption.
Assume that anything you transmit in cleartext over the internet is
not private.  Even if it is not currently being monitored, it is very
possible that it will be in the near future as storage/filtering
capabilities improve.


