Date: Sun, 7 Mar 1993 23:08:23 -0500 (EST)
From: Edward J OConnell <ejo@world.std.com>
Subject: ECON/NANO/PHIL Defending Post-Scarcity

KICKING AT NUMBER 1 POST-SCARCITY POSTULATE 
(Voluntary non-geometric growth expansion):

Many have pointed out that geometric population growth makes Post
Scarcity unlikely. I agree. Here are my reasons for believing in
gradually slackening population expansion. (In a nutshell, I
believe people are slightly more intelligent than bacteria. Or,
perhaps, that they *can* be more intelligent than bacteria, and
someone/something might *make* them be...)

Paraphrase of Post Scarcity Scoffer: "Slowed population growth? No
thanks. I'll take boundless expansion."

Boundless expansion is fun. Until you drown in your own excrement.
(scratch that; expansion stops being fun when you can't get the
stench of your own excrement from your nostrils. And I don't want
an invisible hand selling me nostril filters, either. I want less
excrement.)

I do believe in the eventual conversion of all matter to
intelligence... Underline eventual. A lot of our arguments arise
from differences in interpreting the exponential curve of
knowledge/technological development. I see it slowing down in the
near future, for various reasons. I don't believe in robust
nanotech in our lifetime. That's why I'm being frozen. That's why
I support intelligent environmental regulation. (Flame away at the
perceived oxymoron! But I've got data from people I respect in the
lifesciences that is hard to explain away, philosophically. I've
also encountered some Libertarians who were 'soft' on this
issue...)

My fear is we almost get to Post Scarcity, and the whole
Industrial Machine conks out/dissolves into entropy from Ecocrash.
Scant inches from the tech that would ensure the
races/intelligence's survival till the heat death of the universe,
assuming we can't lick that.

I believe that explosive growth, at the wrong points in time, can
lead to disaster. Easter island. As clever as we currently think
we are, we are still utterly reliant on passive transport of fresh
water by chaotic natural forces for the bulk of our food supply.
Usable arable land has been drastically reduced by
deforestation/erosion/desertification during the last 200 years.
I'm not sure there's enough latent energy in the FreeMarket to see
us through a really large environmental disaster. IMO, this is the
ugly short term reality.

I wasn't kidding about Robert Morris, and the human reproductive
default. You'll notice I wasn't advocating or railing against
him--merely acknowledging his existence. (OK, I said it needed to
be done. I wasn't talking about involuntary sterilization,
although I'm fairly sure we'll see that again in our lifetimes, I
was talking about technological change that ends ACCIDENTAL human
reproduction. It sure would be a part of my little nation. You
would have to look into a woman's eyes, say out loud, "We both
want to have a baby(in unison) and actually BELIEVE it, according
to an internal polygraph, to be able to conceive in my Oniel
colony!)

Radical environmentalists exist. Virus's exist. Genehackers may
one day exist. Interesting things might happen. We all accept a
certain degree of unpleasantness in our discussions of the future.
Just different sorts of unpleasantness, I've noticed.

I honestly believe there is no urge to reproduce BODILY per
se--there's just the urge for sex, and social contact. Once the
economic/cultural reasons for reproduction are removed, birthrates
drop to 'sustainable' levels. (Didn't notice the quotes around
'sustainable' before, huh? No I don't want to live in the woods
and get malaria. I'm typing on a nice Mac II in a nice warm
apartment. My nanobushmen are *comfortable* in the woods, and have
their Macs in their heads.)

